course: history
lesson: 23
title: The Mongols
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: On the wide grasslands of Asia, called the steppe, the Mongols lived as nomads in round felt tents called gers, herding horses, sheep and goats, and children learned to ride at three or four.
explain: In 1206 a leader named Temujin united the Mongol tribes and took the title Genghis Khan; he and his sons and grandsons built the largest land empire in history.
explain: Their armies were fast because every rider brought several horses and swapped to a fresh one when the first got tired, like a relay team that never stops running.
explain: The conquests were terrible for the cities that resisted, and in 1240 the Mongols destroyed Kyiv, but once the fighting was over traders could cross Asia more safely than ever before.
explain: Genghis's grandson Kublai Khan became emperor of China, and a Venetian merchant called Marco Polo wrote a book about his court that amazed Europe.
fact: The Mongols were nomads of the Asian steppe who lived in round felt tents called gers and herded horses, sheep and goats.
fact: Temujin united the Mongol tribes and in 1206 was named Genghis Khan, meaning universal ruler.
fact: Mongol armies moved very fast because each rider had several horses, and they used tricks such as pretending to retreat to lure enemies into traps.
fact: The Mongols used Chinese and Persian engineers to besiege walled cities, and cities that resisted were often destroyed and many people died.
fact: At its biggest the Mongol Empire stretched from Korea and China to Ukraine, Iran and the Middle East, the largest land empire in history.
fact: In 1240 the Mongols destroyed Kyiv, and the lands of Rus paid them tribute, money and goods handed over every year, for about two hundred years.
fact: After the conquests the Mongol lands were peaceful enough for traders to travel safely across Asia; historians call this the Mongol peace, or Pax Mongolica.
fact: The Mongols had relay stations with fresh horses so that messengers could ride hundreds of kilometres a day, showing a metal pass called a paiza.
fact: Genghis's grandson Kublai Khan conquered China and founded the Yuan dynasty, with his capital where Beijing is now.
fact: Kublai Khan's fleets twice tried to invade Japan, in 1274 and 1281, and both attempts failed, partly because of storms.
fact: Marco Polo, a merchant from Venice, said he served Kublai Khan, and his book told Europeans about paper money, coal and huge cities; some historians doubt parts of his story, but much of what he described was accurate.
fact: The Mongols let people of many religions worship freely, including Buddhists, Muslims and Christians, which helped them rule many different peoples.
exercise: What is a ger? | A round felt tent of the steppe nomads | A curved Mongol sword with a horn handle | A small, fast type of horse | A Chinese boat
exercise: What was Genghis Khan's name before he became ruler? | Temujin | Kublai Khan | Marco Polo | Saladin of Egypt
exercise: In which year was Temujin named Genghis Khan? | 1206 | 1066 | 1492 | 1812
exercise: Why did each Mongol rider take several horses? | To swap to a fresh horse and keep moving fast | To sell them in every town they passed through | To carry the gold home | Because horses were holy
exercise: What trick did Mongol armies often use? | Pretending to retreat to lure enemies into a trap | Hiding inside a giant wooden horse | Building castles in one day | Sailing up the rivers in long Viking-style ships
exercise: Who helped the Mongols attack walled cities? | Chinese and Persian engineers | Viking shipbuilders | English longbowmen | Roman legions
exercise: Which lands were part of the Mongol Empire? | China, Iran and Ukraine | England, France and Spain | Australia and New Zealand | Brazil and Mexico
exercise: What happened to Kyiv in 1240? | The Mongols destroyed it | It became the Mongol capital | It was founded | It joined the Roman Empire
exercise: What was the Pax Mongolica? | A time when traders could cross Asia safely | A Mongol warship | A peace treaty with the Vikings, signed in Kyiv in 1240 | A Mongol holiday
exercise: What did a Mongol messenger show to get a fresh horse? | A metal pass called a paiza | A letter from the Pope | A gold crown | A wooden shield
exercise: Which modern city stands where Kublai Khan had his capital? | Beijing | Baghdad in Iraq | Moscow in Russia | Venice in Italy
exercise: What happened when Kublai Khan's fleets tried to invade Japan? | Both attempts failed, partly because of storms | Japan joined the empire at once and paid tribute | Japan invaded Mongolia instead | The fleets never left port
exercise: Where was Marco Polo from? | Venice | Beijing in China | Kyiv in Rus | London in England
exercise: What surprised Europeans in Marco Polo's book? | Paper money, coal and huge cities | Tanks, aeroplanes and big guns | Potatoes and chocolate from America | Kangaroos and koalas
exercise: How did the Mongols treat different religions? | They let people worship freely | They allowed only one religion | They banned all religion | They forced everyone to become Buddhist
test: kid in my class says genghis khan was just a cartoon baddie, not real | He was a real ruler who built a huge empire from 1206 | He was a made-up character from a film | He was a Viking from Norway who sailed east | He was an English king
test: Why would letting everyone keep their own religion help the Mongols rule? | Peoples were less likely to rebel | It made the horses faster | It doubled the gold in the empire | The Mongols had no army
test: A Mongol rider and a knight in heavy armour both set off on a 100 km journey. Who probably arrives first? | The Mongol, swapping to fresh horses | The knight, because armour is faster | They arrive together | Neither, horses cannot go 100 km
test: Why was it easier to carry silk from China to Europe in 1300 than in 1200? | One power controlled most of the Silk Road and kept it safe | Someone built a long motorway across the desert | The sea between them dried up | Silk became lighter
test: What would a messenger without a paiza probably find at a Mongol relay station? | No fresh horse for him | A free feast and a crown | A ship to sail to Japan | A new empire of his own to rule
test: so marco polo made it ALL up yeah? total liar? | Some parts are doubted, but he described many things accurately | Yes, he never left his house | No, every word is proven true | He was really a Mongol prince
test: In a strategy game the Mongols take a city that fought back. What did that really often mean for the people there? | The city was destroyed and many people died | Nothing happened to anyone in the city at all | Everyone was given gold | The people became Mongol generals
test: Two hundred years of paying tribute to the Mongols. Which lands did this happen to? | The lands of Rus, including around Kyiv | England and Wales | The Inca Empire, high in the mountains of Peru | Australia
test: Why did Mongol children learn to ride at three or four? | Their whole way of life on the steppe depended on horses | Horses were their only toys by law | They had no legs strong enough to walk | It was a Chinese custom
test: A friend says the Mongols could not take walled cities, they only fought in fields. What do you say? | They could, using Chinese and Persian engineers | Right, they never even tried to attack a city | Right, they had no weapons | They only fought at sea
exam: Identify the title conferred upon Temujin in the year twelve hundred and six. | Genghis Khan | Kublai Khan, his grandson | A sultan of Egypt | Emperor of Rome
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the circumstances of trade across Asia under the Pax Mongolica? | Merchants could travel with comparative safety | All trade was prohibited | Merchants were obliged to travel by sea around India and Arabia | Trade was conducted solely with Japan
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the city of Kyiv in the year twelve hundred and forty. | It was destroyed by the Mongols | It was made the capital of China | It was founded by Marco Polo | It was untouched by any conflict
exam: Ascertain the principal advantage conferred upon Mongol forces by each rider's possession of several horses. | Sustained speed through the use of fresh mounts | Increased weight of armour | The capacity to cross the ocean | Exemption from the payment of tribute
